---
// Closing beat for the Gadgets section.
//
// Three pillars, in order of importance:
//   1. WebAssembly core — anyone can build a Gadget.
//   2. Optional React/TSX frontends.
//   3. We eat our own dog food: most bundled Gadgets are
//      built on the same system third-party Gadgets use.
//
// Each pillar is anchored by a brand glyph rather than an
// abstract heroicon: the WebAssembly mark (`simple-icons:
// webassembly`) and the React mark (`simple-icons:react`)
// from the simple-icons iconify set, plus a tiny inline
// Snappy for the eat-our-own claim. The marks are coloured
// in their respective brands so the visual immediately reads
// "WASM and React are the technologies, Snappy is us."
//
// A dedicated developer guide lives elsewhere; this section
// only mentions it in a single trailing line.

import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import snappyOriginal from "../../assets/mascots/snappy-original-1024.png";
---

<div class="rounded-[20px] border border-border bg-surface-inset p-8 md:p-12">
  <div class="grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-[1.1fr_1fr] md:items-start md:gap-16">
    <div class="max-w-[520px]">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Open ecosystem</p>
      <h3
        class="text-balance text-3xl font-semibold leading-[1.1] tracking-tight md:text-4xl"
      >
        Gadgets all the way down.
      </h3>
      <p
        class="mt-4 text-pretty text-base leading-relaxed text-text-secondary"
      >
        Torchsnap is a small launcher with a plugin system. Not a
        big launcher with a few extension points bolted on. All
        those bundled Gadgets are written the way you'd write your
        own, and there's nothing a third-party Gadget can't reach
        for that a built-in one can.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-text-muted">
        A dedicated SDK guide lands alongside the public beta. For
        now: peek at the bundled Gadgets in the source repo to see
        the shape of things.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-7">
      {/* WebAssembly — brand purple, square mark with WA glyph.
          The glyph carries enough recognition on its own; no
          frame around it. */}
      <li class="flex items-center gap-5">
        <Icon
          name="simple-icons:webassembly"
          class="h-10 w-10 shrink-0"
          style="color: #654FF0;"
          aria-label="WebAssembly"
        />
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <div class="text-base font-semibold text-text-primary">
            WebAssembly core
          </div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            Any language that compiles to wasm32-wasip2 works as a
            Gadget: Rust, Go, JavaScript, Zig. Each runs in its own
            sandbox, isolated from the host and from every other
            Gadget.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      {/* React — brand cyan, atom mark. Same dimensions as the
          WASM mark so the column reads as a coherent triplet. */}
      <li class="flex items-center gap-5">
        <Icon
          name="simple-icons:react"
          class="h-10 w-10 shrink-0"
          style="color: #61DAFB;"
          aria-label="React"
        />
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <div class="text-base font-semibold text-text-primary">
            Optional React frontends
          </div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            When a Gadget needs more than the standard result list,
            it can ship its own React/TSX surface: settings panels,
            custom launcher views, inline strips above the results.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      {/* Snappy — the dog-food beat. The mascot itself is the
          icon: small, sized to match the brand glyphs above so
          the row keeps its rhythm. */}
      <li class="flex items-center gap-5">
        <Image
          src={snappyOriginal}
          alt=""
          width={56}
          height={56}
          densities={[1, 2, 3]}
          format="webp"
          loading="lazy"
          class="h-10 w-10 shrink-0 select-none object-contain"
        />
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <div class="text-base font-semibold text-text-primary">
            We use what we ship
          </div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
            Most of Torchsnap's own bundled Gadgets are written the
            same way you'd write one. No side door, no shortcut,
            just the same way in for everyone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>
